# Ophthalmology Dataset Tagging Standard

This document defines the standardized tagging schema used in the
OphthalmologyDatasets repository.

A dataset may have multiple values under each tag dimension.
Whenever possible, existing terms should be reused instead of creating
new synonymous tags.

---

## 1. Modality

Describes how the ophthalmic data were acquired.

- Fundus Photography
- Ultra-Widefield Fundus (UWF)
- OCT
- OCTA
- Fluorescein Angiography (FA)
- Fundus Autofluorescence (FAF)
- Slit-Lamp Photography
- Corneal Microscopy
- Surgical Microscopy
- Ultrasound
- Visual Field / Perimetry
- Other

Example:

Modality: Fundus Photography, OCT

---

## 2. Anatomy

Describes the anatomical structure or region represented in the dataset.

- Retina
- Retinal Vessel
- Macula / Fovea
- Optic Disc / Cup
- Choroid
- Cornea
- Iris
- Lens
- Anterior Chamber
- Vitreous
- Optic Nerve
- Multiple
- Other

Example:

Anatomy: Retina, Optic Disc / Cup

---

## 3. Disease / Condition

Describes the disease or clinical condition represented in the dataset.

- Healthy / Normal
- Glaucoma
- Diabetic Retinopathy
- Diabetic Macular Edema
- Age-related Macular Degeneration (AMD)
- Cataract
- Myopia
- Pathologic Myopia
- Retinal Detachment
- Keratoconus
- Corneal Disease
- Other Retinal Disease
- Multi-disease
- Other

Example:

Disease / Condition: Glaucoma

---

## 4. Task

Describes the research or machine-learning tasks supported by the dataset.

- Classification
- Disease Detection
- Grading
- Segmentation
- Object Detection
- Localization
- Registration
- Tracking
- Image Quality Assessment
- Progression Prediction
- Treatment Response Prediction
- Image Generation
- Retrieval
- Visual Question Answering (VQA)
- Report / Text Generation
- Surgical Phase Recognition
- Other

Example:

Task: Classification, Segmentation

---

## 5. Data Type

Describes the computational form of the provided data.

- 2D Image
- 3D Volume
- Video
- Visual Field
- Text
- Tabular / Clinical Data
- Other

Example:

Data Type: 2D Image, Tabular / Clinical Data

---

## 6. Annotation Type

Describes the type of ground-truth annotation provided by the dataset.

- Class Label
- Diagnosis
- Grade
- Pixel Mask
- Voxel Mask
- Bounding Box
- Landmark / Keypoint
- Temporal Label
- Registration Pair / Correspondence
- Clinical Measurement
- Report / Text
- Other

Example:

Annotation Type: Diagnosis, Pixel Mask

---

## 7. Data Structure

Describes how samples or modalities within the dataset are organized.

- Cross-sectional
- Longitudinal
- Paired
- Multi-view
- Multimodal
- Temporal Sequence
- Other

Example:

Data Structure: Longitudinal, Multimodal

---

## 8. Clinical Setting

Describes the clinical context in which the data were acquired or intended
to be used.

- Screening
- Diagnosis
- Treatment
- Follow-up
- Surgery
- Research / General
- Other

Example:

Clinical Setting: Screening

---

# Dataset Metadata

The following fields describe dataset metadata and should not be treated
as classification tags.

- Dataset Name
- Full Name
- Year
- Dataset Size
- Number of Patients
- Number of Eyes
- Country / Region
- Institution
- Access
- License
- Dataset URL
- Paper
- Code

## Access

Use one of the following standardized access labels:

- Open
- Registration Required
- Request Required
- Challenge / Competition Access
- Restricted
- Unavailable
- Unknown

---

# Tagging Principles

1. Multiple tags are allowed within each dimension.

2. Use the standardized vocabulary whenever possible.

3. Do not create synonymous tags.
   For example, use "Fundus Photography" instead of separately using
   "Fundus", "Fundus Image", and "Color Fundus Image".

4. Use the full disease name rather than abbreviations in metadata.
   For example:
   - Diabetic Retinopathy instead of DR
   - Age-related Macular Degeneration instead of AMD
